  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing the Structure**\n\nOkay, I\'m now focusing on the requested clean architecture. I\'m going to start brainstorming how the Flutter project structure will support this design. I\'m also now going to work on generating the required Flutter project structure.\n\n\n**Defining the Models**\n\nI\'ve defined the core domain models, including `Medicine`, `Reminder`, `ComplianceLog`, `FamilyMember`, and `UserProfile`, each with relevant attributes. Now, I\'m thinking through how to structure the database and persistence layer, considering Room for efficient data management. I\'m starting to think about `MedicineDao`, `ReminderDao`, and `ProfileDao` to interact with this data.\n\n\n**Implementing Offline Persistence**\n\nI\'m now focusing on ensuring the app operates flawlessly offline. My current thinking revolves around `Preferences` to store profile and settings data, which is key for providing real offline capability to back up the REST API layer. I\'m excited about the offline-first approach now!\n\n\n**Envisioning the OCR Feature**\n\nI\'m currently captivated by the idea of using pre-selected mock prescription images, or a visual prompt to take one, and feeding them directly into the Gemini model. This allows extraction of medicines, dosages, and timings! The Gemini API implementation seems extremely promising. Plus, I will include medicine interaction analysis, flagging duplicate medicines or warnings. This will also provide AI-powered reminder suggestions! I\'m now structuring the viewmodels to manage all of the new states.\n\n\n**Visualizing the UI Screens**\n\nI\'m now deep in the UI design. I\'m focusing on Jetpack Compose and Jetpack Navigation for a fluid user experience. The minimalist login screen, with Google, Apple, and OTP options, is coming together. The main dashboard will feature active meds, upcoming doses, and compliance at a glance, and will link to a beautiful interactive calendar. The prescription upload flow will integrate Gemini OCR, allowing for edit, delete, and add on parsed medication cards.\n\n\n**Building the Feature Set**\n\nI\'m now deep in the feature implementation phase! I\'m focused on the pricing structure, specifically \"Pay Per Day\", incorporating a PayU payment modal simulation. The goal is to clearly show the Pro features unlocked, such as custom voice reminder recording, WhatsApp alert configuration, and the family monitor screen. I\'m also planning the new screens, including the Family Members screen, Settings screen, and most excitingly, an Admin simulation configuration overlay for testing admin updates in real-time. I will next focus on creating the directory structures.\n\n\n**Structuring the Project**\n\nOkay, I\'m now thinking about how to create the project\'s directory structure systematically. I\'m focusing on defining component packages such as `data.model`, `data.local`, `data.api`, `data.ai`, and `ui`. I will begin by constructing the domain models, and then build the file structure step-by-step.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will create the domain models file `/app/src/main/java/com/example/data/model/Models.kt` containing all the data entities used in \"MediReminder AI\" (Medicines, Reminder Logs, Family Members, User Profiles, and real-time Admin Configuration settings) decorated for Moshi serialization and direct Room persistence mapping."
